{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-2/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, distinguish the motif's bounding-box origin from the motif coordinate aligned with a marker. Represent that alignment as a signed offset and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. Replay every marker footprint against the complete demo output, including source motif retention, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A common failure is to shift a plausible template by intuition (top-left, center, or a salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. After incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and re-derive the offset from observed coordinate correspondences. Validate serialization independently: row widths and legal values must be checked before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-2/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/session-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Session note: distinguish invalid grids from incorrect answers

In a Continual-ARC interaction, feedback may distinguish a malformed/invalid ARC grid from a valid grid that simply fails the task. A wrong submission in this session was labeled “not a valid ARC grid”; subsequent candidate construction still failed, underscoring two separate checks: validate serialization and dimensions first, then reassess the inferred task rule based on ordinary incorrect feedback.

Before submitting, verify mechanically that the payload is one JSON action object; grid is nonempty, each row is a list of integer colors 0–9, all rows have the same positive width, and height/width are each 1–30. Check every row. For task inference, compare demonstrated input/output dimensions and full cell deltas before assuming a same-size transformation. A structurally valid candidate is not thereby conceptually correct.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-2/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/session-serialization-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/continual-turn-lessons.md", "content": "# Continual ARC turn completeness and evidence boundaries

In continual puzzle interfaces, each message—including incorrect feedback—requires one legal action JSON on the final line. A thoughtful diagnosis without a valid action leaves the turn incomplete.

After a rejection, use references from previously encountered sessions only as process cautions. They are not evidence that this task shares the referenced transformation; do not transplant specific rules or guessed output grids. Reconstruct the current examples' deltas and representations, and demand exact replay of every available demo before another submission. If no supported candidate exists, request a demo only when it answers a concrete ambiguity that could change the output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-2/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/continual-turn-lessons.md"}
